<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hama is a city on the Orontes river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, famous for its great wooden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheels</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noria">norias</a>. As Hamath it was the capital of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdom until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> annexed it in 720 BCE, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was ruled by a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>, including the historian-prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abu al-Fida</a>. In 1982 the Syrian government crushed an uprising there, killing thousands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hama.jpg" alt="Hama" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hama,_Panoramic_view,_Syria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It extinguished most of what remained, although <b>Hama</b> survived as a Mamluk vassal into the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muslim Brotherhood's uprising of 1976–1982 ended in the <b>Hama</b> massacre of 1982, the single deadliest act of violence by any state on its own people in modern Arab history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Horns of <b>Hama</b> on 13 April 1175 Saladin's outnumbered veterans surrounded and crushed the combined armies of Mosul and Aleppo; at the Sultan's Mound in 1176 the Zengids broke his left wing before his charge routed them, and Saif ad-Din barely escaped.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Kipchak boy captured in a Mongol invasion, was sold at the slave market of Sivas and again at <b>Hama</b>, came into the service of the sultan as-Salih Ayyub and in 1260 seized the throne, which he held until 1277; Barquq, a Circassian mamluk of the amir Yalbugha, became…</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Imber emigrated to Ottoman-ruled Palestine in 1882 and read the poem to the pioneers of the early Jewish villages, among them Rishon LeZion, Rehovot, Gedera and Yesud <b>Hama</b>'ala.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a></li><li><blockquote>Victory gave him Damascus, Homs and <b>Hama</b>; he then proclaimed himself king, and the Abbasid caliph named him Sultan of Egypt and Syria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
